<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · c. 320 BCE – 550 CE (the Maurya and Gupta empires)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</h1><p class="soc-sub">From King to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lion-capital-of-ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lion-capital-of-ashoka.jpg" alt="Lion Capital of Ashoka"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lion-capital-of-ashoka">Lion Capital of Ashoka</a><span class="soc-credit">Chrisi1964 · CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">The Brahmanical law books of this age, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmashastra">Dharmashastras</a> such as the Manusmriti, ranked people in four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> fixed by birth: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a>, Vaishyas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>, with groups such as the Chandalas placed outside them. This was a Brahmin ideal, and it was contested. Buddhist texts put the Kshatriyas first and described society as householders, and the Greek envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>, around 300 BCE, counted seven hereditary classes. Lived society was made of many jatis, groups born to an occupation that married among themselves. Birth and calling, more than wealth, set a person’s rank, and the law books gave the three “twice-born” varnas rights that Shudras and outcastes lacked. Historians debate how rigid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> was, and how far British rule, much later, hardened it; genetic studies suggest that, at least in the north, marriage within one’s group became the rule only in the first half of the first millennium CE.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> c. 320 BCE – 550 CE (the Maurya and Gupta empires)</span><span><b>People</b> 15–30 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 8</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, <a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n">The king</span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-unk" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Brahmins</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-unk" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Buddhist and Jain monks and nuns</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-unk" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Kshatriyas</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-unk" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Vaishyas</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-most" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:100%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Shudras</span><span class="soc-bar-s">Most people (the rest)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-outcast soc-unk" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Chandalas and other outcastes</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-unk" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:0%"><span class="soc-bar-n">Enslaved people</span><span class="soc-bar-s">No reliable count</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people; ranks no one has counted are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/samudragupta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">The king <span class="soc-term">rājan, mahārājādhirāja</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Indian kings took part of every harvest as tax and were expected to protect their people.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The law books made kingship the duty of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a>: to protect his subjects, punish wrongdoers and uphold the order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>, in return for a share of the harvest, traditionally a sixth. The Maurya kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> with a council of ministers, sent princes to govern the provinces and kept a great standing army, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>’ count 600,000 infantry, 30,000 cavalry and 9,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> (r. c. 268–232 BCE) had edicts carved across his realm urging respect for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and ascetics and proper treatment of slaves and servants. The Gupta kings took the title maharajadhiraja, “great king of kings”, and performed the Vedic horse sacrifice; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allahabad-pillar-inscription">Allahabad Pillar Inscription</a> praised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> as a conqueror and a poet. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around 400 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a>, reported that the king ruled without executions: most criminals were only fined, and repeated rebels lost their right hand.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Kingship was meant for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a>, but few dynasties of the age were Kshatriyas by birth: the Nandas were said to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>, the Shungas and Kanvas were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, and the Gupta records never name their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>. From the 4th century Brahmins gave rising dynasties Kshatriya standing by tracing their descent to the solar and lunar lines of the epics.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One ruler, with the royal family and the women of the palace.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">2</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Brahmins <span class="soc-term">brāhmaṇa</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Brahmins were priests and teachers who learned the holy texts called the Vedas by heart.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The Brahmanical texts placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> first. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a> and Vaishyas they were “twice-born”, initiated into the study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, which were learned by heart; but only Brahmins could teach the Vedas, perform sacrifices for others and live on gifts. The Manusmriti told kings never to tax a learned Brahmin and never to put a Brahmin to death, whatever his crime. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> counted Brahmins among his “philosophers”, few in number but the most honoured, exempt from public duties and hired to perform sacrifices and funeral rites. In practice many Brahmins lived as farmers or officials, and some as generals, and Buddhist texts show them in many trades. The Maurya kings patronised Buddhists, Jains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika">Ajivikas</a> as well as Brahmins; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> favoured Brahmins, performing Vedic sacrifices and granting them villages, with their revenue and free of tax, by charters engraved on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates. Brahmins wrote the law books, and Brahmin ministers served kings.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> In the law books <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> rank came by birth alone. A Brahmin in hard times was allowed to live by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a>’s or a Vaishya’s work, and one who committed a grievous sin could be cast out of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No count survives: Megasthenes wrote only that his class of “philosophers”, Brahmins and ascetics, was small in number but the most honoured.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmashastra">Dharmashastra</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ajivika.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Buddhist and Jain monks and nuns <span class="soc-term">bhikkhu, bhikkhunī, śramaṇa, saṅgha</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Buddhist and Jain monks and nuns gave up their homes to live simply and study their faith.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Buddhist and Jain monks and nuns, and the ascetics of smaller orders such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika">Ajivikas</a>, left household life and stood outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> order: the Buddhist order took people of every varna, though not slaves or debtors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>’s edicts paired “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and shramanas” as the holy men to be honoured, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> counted ascetics with the Brahmins among his “philosophers”. Monks and nuns shaved their heads, wore robes and owned little beyond them and an alms bowl; Buddhist monks lived by a code of more than two hundred rules, kept celibate and ate only before noon. Kings, merchants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> gave the orders caves, monasteries and land: Ashoka gave the Ajivikas caves in the Barabar Hills and, by Sri Lankan tradition, sent his son Mahinda and his daughter Sanghamitta to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a>, who favoured Brahmins but tolerated other faiths, great monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> became centres of learning, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Huna</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> destroyed many others in the early 6th century.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Anyone free of debt and servitude could take the robe, whatever their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>, and a monk or nun could leave the order and return to household life.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: monks and nuns were never counted across India.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahinda-buddhist-monk">Mahinda (Buddhist monk)</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashvaghosha">Ashvaghosha</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika">Ajivika</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">4</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Kshatriyas <span class="soc-term">kṣatriya, rājanya</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Kshatriyas were warriors and rulers whose duty was to protect the people.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a> were the warrior and ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>. The law books told them to protect the people, fight and govern, to study the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, sacrifice and give gifts, but not to teach the Vedas or live on gifts. Kingship belonged to them in theory, and the clans that ruled the old republics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a>, called themselves Kshatriyas and were patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>. Buddhist texts placed the Kshatriyas above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. Kshatriya lineages held land, commanded armies and governed provinces, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> many clan states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> claimed Kshatriya status. Brahmin writers counted the foreign conquerors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavanas</a>) and Shakas, as Kshatriyas who had fallen to the rank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a> by neglecting the rites. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> described a hereditary class of soldiers, the second most numerous, paid by the state and idle in peacetime, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a> expected troops of every varna.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> rank came by birth, but success could claim it: from the 4th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> gave newly risen royal families Kshatriya genealogies, while a Kshatriya people that abandoned the rites could be counted as fallen.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No count survives. Megasthenes thought soldiers the second most numerous of his seven classes, but soldiers came from every varna.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-5"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Vaishyas <span class="soc-term">vaiśya, śreṣṭhin, sārthavāha</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Vaishyas were traders, bankers and farmers, and many of them belonged to guilds.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The Vaishyas were the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> and, like the two above them, twice-born, entitled to study the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>. The law books gave them farming, herding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, trade and lending money, though the Manusmriti spends only a handful of verses on them and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>. Over these centuries the name came to mean above all merchants and moneylenders, who prospered on the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> cities and the ports. Traders and craftsmen formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> (shrenis) with their own laws, which the Manusmriti told kings to respect. Guilds took deposits, lent money and paid for monuments: an inscription credits the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> carvers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> with work on the southern gateway of the great stupa at Sanchi. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> a district governor was advised by a council of the chief merchant, the chief caravan trader, the chief artisan and the chief scribe. Many merchants followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> saw the heads of Vaishya families running houses that gave food and medicine to the poor and sick.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> came by birth, but trade could make a family rich and powerful: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> heads sat on councils and advised kings, and some historians think the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta dynasty</a> itself was of Vaishya origin.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No count survives for any varna.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">6</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>Most people (the rest)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Shudras <span class="soc-term">śūdra</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Shudras were farmers, workers and craftspeople, and old law books said they should serve the higher groups.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a> were the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>, born, the law books said, to serve the three twice-born varnas. They were barred from studying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and from Vedic sacrifice, and a Shudra was punished more harshly for offences against a person of higher varna. The Manusmriti held that a Shudra was created for servitude, should not pile up wealth and might have his goods taken by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>. Yet the texts disagree with one another, and Shudras were freer of the many ritual rules that bound the twice-born. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a> counted Shudras among the free Aryas, who could not be sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and allowed them to serve as soldiers. In practice Shudras were peasants, artisans, labourers and servants, and by the 7th century the Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> described the Shudras as the farmers. Some grew rich, and the Nanda kings who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> were said to be Shudras.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> in the texts, but some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a> grew rich and powerful, and an outcaste group that took up farming could be absorbed into the Shudras.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers, since no count of any varna survives: in the texts the labouring people, and by the 7th century, Xuanzang wrote, the farmers. This share also holds the Brahmins, monks and nuns, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas, Chandalas and dasas, whose numbers are unknown.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-outcast" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">7</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Outcast groups</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Chandalas and other outcastes <span class="soc-term">caṇḍāla, śvapaca</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Chandalas were made to live apart from others and did work such as carrying away the dead.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Below and outside the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> the texts placed groups they held to be impure, above all the Chandalas, along with the Shvapachas and peoples such as the Nishadas. The law books explained the Chandala as the child of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a> father and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> mother, the most condemned of mixed unions. The Manusmriti ordered that Chandalas live outside the village, own only dogs and donkeys, wear the clothes of the dead and eat from broken dishes, and gave them the work of carrying away the corpses of people with no kin and executing condemned criminals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a>, around 400, found that the Chandalas lived apart; when they entered a town or a market they struck a piece of wood so that others could avoid touching them, and only they hunted, fished and sold meat. Historians such as Vivekanand Jha see untouchability spreading over these centuries and reaching its height after 600; Patrick Olivelle notes that the law books speak of the impurity of individuals rather than of whole varnas.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The texts made the status hereditary, but over generations groups could move: a forest people that settled to farming could be absorbed among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No count survives.</p></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic soc-rank-ph" aria-hidden="true">8</span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>No reliable count</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t">Enslaved people <span class="soc-term">dāsa, dāsī</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay, often as servants in homes.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> existed throughout these centuries, but historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romila-thapar">Romila Thapar</a> note that slaves were not the main labour force, as they were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>; most served in households as servants, nurses and attendants, and some worked the land beside hired labourers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> had no slaves, perhaps because slaves there could buy back their freedom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>’s edicts urged proper treatment of slaves and servants, and his edict on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> war records 150,000 people deported. The Manusmriti lists seven kinds of slave: the war captive, the one who serves for food, the house-born, the bought, the given, the inherited and the one enslaved as punishment. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a> forbade selling a free Arya into slavery, though a man in distress might pledge himself. It let slaves keep their earnings and inheritance, and an owner who abused a slave, or forced himself on a slave woman, lost the price he had paid; she went free.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came by the owner’s grant or by paying one’s price, a right the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a> recognised; a free man pledged for a debt was released when it was repaid.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> No reliable count: slaves were never counted, and historians such as Romila Thapar hold that they were not the main labour force.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a></div></div></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="classical-india">Be born in classical India</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>In theory birth fixed a person’s place, but power was open to newcomers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, whose origins are obscure, overthrew the Nanda kings, who were themselves said to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>. Around 185 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga">Pushyamitra Shunga</a> killed the last Maurya king and founded his own dynasty, and the Gupta records never name the family’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>. Brahmins gave rising royal families <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> genealogies, and the law books fitted foreign conquerors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and Shakas, into the scheme as fallen Kshatriyas. New groups formed as the state grew: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayastha">Kayasthas</a>, scribes and record-keepers, appear as officials in Gupta inscriptions. Wealth mattered too: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> heads sat on councils, and some Shudras grew rich. The Buddhist and Jain orders took members of every varna, though the Buddhists refused slaves and debtors, and a forest people that settled to farming could join the Shudras. Falls were real as well: the law books cast out of their varna those who committed grievous sins. Genetic and textual evidence suggests that the barriers hardened over these centuries.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>A woman’s rank followed her father’s and then her husband’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>. The law books placed her under the protection of father, husband and sons, and the Manusmriti declared that a woman should never be independent, though it let her own property, such as wedding gifts; Tim Dyson sees women’s rights declining in the Indo-Aryan heartland from Mauryan times. Royal women could hold real power: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaradevi">Kumaradevi</a> appears beside her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-i">Chandragupta I</a> on Gupta gold coins, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a>’s daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabhavatigupta">Prabhavatigupta</a> ruled the Vakataka kingdom as regent for about twenty years from around 390, issuing land grants in her own name. Buddhist nuns had an order of their own, though bound by more rules than monks, and tradition says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>’s daughter Sanghamitta took it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> noted women studying philosophy among the ascetics. Widow-burning was rare: the first Indian inscription recording one, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eran">Eran</a>, dates from 510. Enslaved women served in royal households.</p></section>
