he Vedas by heart.","rise":"In the law books Brahmin rank came by birth alone. A Brahmin in hard times was allowed to live by a Kshatriya’s or a Vaishya’s work, and one who committed a grievous sin could be cast out of his varna.","why":"The Brahmanical texts placed Brahmins first.","people":[["chanakya","Chanakya"]]},{"n":3,"lv":2,"band":"religious","name":"Buddhist and Jain monks and nuns","term":"bhikkhu, bhikkhunī, śramaṇa, saṅgha","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":0,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ajivika.jpg","you":"You have left your family, shaved your head and taken the robe; you own little more than your robes and an alms bowl, eat only before noon and live by the rules of your order.","kid":"Buddhist and Jain monks and nuns gave up their homes to live simply and study their faith.","rise":"Anyone free of debt and servitude could take the robe, whatever their varna, and a monk or nun could leave the order and return to household life.","why":"Buddhist and Jain monks and nuns, and the ascetics of smaller orders such as the Ajivikas, left household life and stood outside the varna order: the Buddhist order took people of every varna, though not slaves or…","people":[["mahinda-buddhist-monk","Mahinda (Buddhist monk)"],["ashvaghosha","Ashvaghosha"]]},{"n":4,"lv":3,"band":"nobility","name":"Kshatriyas","term":"kṣatriya, rājanya","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":0,"pic":"","you":"You were born to a warrior lineage: the law books tell you to fight, govern and protect, to study the Vedas and give gifts, and you may command troops or govern a province.","kid":"Kshatriyas were warriors and rulers whose duty was to protect the people.","rise":"Kshatriya rank came by birth, but success could claim it: from the 4th century Brahmins gave newly risen royal families Kshatriya genealogies, while a Kshatriya people that abandoned the rites could be counted as fallen.","why":"The Kshatriyas were the warrior and ruling varna.","people":[]},{"n":5,"lv":4,"band":"commoners","name":"Vaishyas","term":"vaiśya, śreṣṭhin, sārthavāha","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":1,"pic":"","you":"You trade or lend money, belong to a guild that has its own laws and takes deposits, and as one of the twice-born you may study the Vedas.","kid":"Vaishyas were traders, bankers and farmers, and many of them belonged to guilds.","rise":"The varna came by birth, but trade could make a family rich and powerful: guild heads sat on councils and advised kings, and some historians think the Gupta dynasty itself was of Vaishya origin.","why":"The Vaishyas were the third varna and, like the two above them, twice-born, entitled to study the Vedas.","people":[]},{"n":6,"lv":5,"band":"commoners","name":"Shudras","term":"śūdra","lbl":"Most people (the rest)","w":100,"firm":1,"most":1,"pic":"","you":"You farm, labour or work at a craft; the law books say you were born to serve the twice-born and may not study the Vedas, but they leave you freer of their ritual rules.","kid":"Shudras were farmers, workers and craftspeople, and old law books said they should serve the higher groups.","rise":"Birth fixed the varna in the texts, but some Shudras grew rich and powerful, and an outcaste group that took up farming could be absorbed into the Shudras.","why":"The Shudras were the fourth varna, born, the law books said, to serve the three twice-born varnas.","people":[]},{"n":7,"lv":6,"band":"outcast","name":"Chandalas and other outcastes","term":"caṇḍāla, śvapaca","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":1,"pic":"","you":"You live outside the village, carry away the dead or hunt and fish for a living, and when you enter a town or a market you must strike a piece of wood so that others can avoid your touch.","kid":"Chandalas were made to live apart from others and did work such as carrying away the dead.","rise":"The texts made the status hereditary, but over generations groups could move: a forest people that settled to farming could be absorbed among the Shudras.","why":"Below and outside the four varnas the texts placed groups they held to be impure, above all the Chandalas, along with the Shvapachas and peoples such as the Nishadas.","people":[]},{"n":8,"lv":6,"band":"enslaved","name":"Enslaved people","term":"dāsa, dāsī","lbl":"No reliable count","w":null,"firm":1,"pic":"","you":"You were captured in war, born in the house or bought; you serve your owner’s household, but you may keep what you earn and, if you can pay your price, buy back your freedom.","kid":"Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay, often as servants in homes.","rise":"Freedom came by the owner’s grant or by paying one’s price, a right the Arthashastra recognised; a free man pledged for a debt was released when it was repaid.","why":"Slavery existed throughout these centuries, but historians such as Romila Thapar note that slaves were not the main labour force, as they were in Greece; most served in households as servants, nurses and attendants…","people":[]}]}</script>
